    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey im studying for my circuits final and im a bit stuck. can u explain how a capacitor acts differently in a dc circuit vs an ac circuit? also what's the formula for capacitive reactance? thanks",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In a DC circuit, a capacitor eventually acts like an open circuit because it charges to the same voltage as the source, blocking any further current flow after briefly charging up. Conversely, in an AC circuit, the voltage constantly changes direction, forcing the capacitor to continuously charge and discharge, which allows an alternating current to pass through effectively. The opposition this impedance poses to AC current is called capacitive reactance, and it is calculated using the formula $X_C = \\frac{1}{2\\pi fC}$, where $f$ is the frequency and $C$ is the capacitance. You will find that as the frequency of the AC signal increases, this reactance decreases, making it easier for the circuit to accept high-frequency signals. Good luck with your final!",
      "source": "target",
